Before unplugging the drive:

The system boots normally into Windows/Linux.
In BIOS/UEFI, the SSD/HDD is listed under Storage/SATA Information or NVMe Information.
The operating system can access the drive normally.

After disconnecting the SATA/NVMe drive and starting the PC:

BIOS/UEFI may show “No SATA device detected,” “NVMe drive not detected,” or the drive may simply disappear from the storage list.
If it is the boot drive, the system may display messages such as “No bootable device,” “Boot device not found,” or “Operating system not found.”
A system with another bootable drive may still start normally, but the disconnected drive will be missing from the operating system.

After reconnecting the drive:

Enter BIOS/UEFI and check that the SSD/HDD appears again under the storage devices.
Boot into the operating system and verify that the drive is visible in File Explorer/Disk Management (Windows) or the appropriate disk utility.
Open a few files or run a disk check to confirm that the drive is functioning correctly.
